==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈtɪtə(ɹ)/|a=UK}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈtɪtɚ/|[ˈtɪɾɚ]|a=GA}}
* {{audio|en|En-au-titter.ogg|a=AU}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪtə(ɹ)|s=2}}

===Etymology 1===
First attested in the 1610s. Probably from {{inh|en|enm|*titeren}}, {{m|enm|*titren}} (attested in {{cog|enm|titering|t=hesitation, vacillation}}), probably a frequentative of {{der|en|enm|titten|t=to waver}}, related to  {{cog|non|titra|t=to shake, shiver, quiver}}, dialectal {{cog|sv|tittra|t=to snicker}}.<ref>{{R:Dictionary.com}}</ref><ref>{{R:Etymonline}}</ref>

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# To [[laugh]] or [[giggle]] in a somewhat [[subdue]]d or restrained way, as from nervousness or poorly-suppressed amusement.
#* '''1863''', {{w|Henry Wadsworth Longfellow}}, ''{{w|Tales of a Wayside Inn}}'' Part First: The Sicilian's Tale - ''[[s:Tales of a Wayside Inn/Part First/The Sicilian's Tale/King Robert of Sicily|King Robert of Sicily]]''
#*: A group of '''tittering''' pages ran before.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1971|author=[[w:Richard Carpenter (screenwriter)|Richard Carpenter]]|title=Catweazle and the Magic Zodiac|publisher=Puffin Books|location=Harmondsworth|page=70|text="Thou coxy, cackling candle!" said Catweazle. "Why dost thou '''titter'''?"}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1997|author=w:Haruki Murakami|translator=w:Jay Rubin|title=w:The Wind-Up Bird Chronicle|location=New York|publisher=Alfred A. Knopf, Inc.|location2=New York|publisher2=Vintage Books|year2=1998|isbn2=0679775439|page2=363|passage=Nor had the joke been a vulgar one: it was the kind of elegant pleasantry that the minister of foreign affairs might have told the crown prince at a garden party a generation ago, causing the surrounding listeners to '''titter''' with delight.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To [[teeter]]; to [[seesaw]].

